WEBVTT

00:00:00.604 --> 00:00:23.100
<v Riley>Hey there, good to have you. Today on the show we've got Quesnel's new mayor, Coralee Oakes, sworn in Tuesday, and the Federal Court challenge from the St'át'imc Chiefs Council over Ottawa's pipeline designation. We also get into the iPhone Duo hinge cracks Apple hasn't explained yet, and whether a repair rule in the EU actually reaches a buyer in Horsefly. Stick around.

00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:56.543
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Wednesday, November 4th, 2026. Today's theme is Repair Culture &amp; Practical Tech. Here in the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:57.543 --> 00:01:26.047
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're looking at what you can fix, keep running, and pass on, and who decides whether you're allowed to. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's mainly clear and around freezing, with a high of 10 today and light drizzle through the day, about a 31 percent chance of precipitation. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around minus 1 and clear, Williams Lake is around freezing and clear, Quesnel is around minus 1 and clear, and Tatla Lake is around 1 with fog.

00:01:26.247 --> 00:01:27.103
<v Riley>On to the news.

00:01:28.203 --> 00:01:34.947
<v Riley>Most of today's stories are close to home, and most of the rest are about things that break and the people who fix them.

00:01:36.562 --> 00:02:06.458
<v Riley>Quesnel has a new council. Coralee Oakes took the oath of office as mayor on Tuesday night, sworn in by Judge Jonathan Hutchison. Returning councillors Tony Goulet, Mitch Vik, Laurey-Anne Roodenburg and Scott Elliott are joined by newcomers Jeremy Drew and Ryan Adams. Oakes says she'll tackle public safety in her first 100 days. Quesnel is one of the larger Cariboo centres, with roughly 9,000 residents, so the first few months of this council will be watched closely.

00:02:08.029 --> 00:02:50.779
<v Casey>Several First Nations are asking the Federal Court to review Ottawa's decision to list a proposed new oil pipeline to B.C.'s coast as a project of national interest. The St'át'imc Chiefs Council, representing 11 communities, says the decision was made in an unlawful and unconstitutional manner. Ts'kw'aylaxw Chief Justin Kane says the project would touch hundreds of rivers and streams and is expected to double crude tanker traffic off the southern coast. Kane says the project was approved without any impact assessment, and without consultation with the St'át'imc Nation as a whole. Other B.C. and Alberta First Nations have applied for judicial review too, and the Tsartlip First Nation says it hasn't been given enough information about the project's impacts.

00:02:51.229 --> 00:02:57.581
<v Riley>That's a legal fight about whether consultation happened, not about whether the pipeline is a good idea.

00:02:59.185 --> 00:03:30.503
<v Riley>A Williams Lake columnist went out on the Golden Buckle Ultra on October 10th. Over 50 people hunted four golden buckles hidden in and around Williams Lake, and took a selfie at each one. Recreation Program Coordinator Maria McKee says the October event grew out of the original Golden Buckle Hunt, a five-week spring adventure that runs from May into early June. The columnist's point was less about the buckles than the people, and how the day felt like a community gathering spread across the local trails.

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:39.885
<v Casey>A tech enthusiast claims the oldest GUI application still running on Windows 11 is CDPLAYER.EXE, a CD player dated November 1992.

00:03:41.493 --> 00:03:59.941
<v Riley>Pneumatic fasteners aren't interchangeable the way screws are. Each nailer takes a specific fastener, and the wrong pairing can split stock or hold poorly. Popular Woodworking's piece is about brad nails, finish nails, pin nails and narrow crown staples, and why you pick the one that matches the tool.

00:04:01.523 --> 00:04:38.145
<v Casey>Building on what we covered on October 28th, Apple has told its stores and authorized service providers to inspect iPhone Duo foldables with cracked inner displays, and to repair or replace them under standard procedures. Owners began reporting hinge cracks within days of the phone going on sale on October 23rd. Apple has announced no recall and no public repair program, and it hasn't disclosed how many phones are affected. A teardown by iFixit points to the hinge, but Apple hasn't confirmed a cause. IDC's Francisco Jeronimo says it's too early to judge the scale, and that defects are common when a new product launches.

00:04:38.595 --> 00:04:43.259
<v Riley>No count and no cause. Those are the two numbers that tell you how much to worry.

00:04:44.973 --> 00:05:07.533
<v Riley>The Muzi Works Base Uno is a small, pre-flashed Meshtastic board with a solar input and a 1,500 mAh battery. In a first test the reviewer connected it over Bluetooth, found nearby nodes, ran a traceroute, and charged the battery to around 96 percent from a small solar panel. There's no screen on the board, so configuration happens through the Meshtastic app.

00:05:08.973 --> 00:05:35.261
<v Casey>A teardown of a purportedly IP44-rated solar light sold in a Czech supermarket found an unprotected 18650 lithium-ion cell where a NiMH cell would normally sit. There's no charge management and no cell protection. The 5.5-volt solar panel was charging the cell at around 6 volts, and in an outdoor test the cell reached 4.8 volts before the sun went behind a building. NiMH cells can take that kind of abuse. Lithium-ion can't.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:41.302
<v Riley>4.8 volts on a cell that's supposed to stop around 4.2. That's the number to remember.

00:05:43.031 --> 00:06:10.599
<v Riley>University of Massachusetts Amherst researchers have built an ultrathin, flexible electronic mesh that grows into living human heart cells. As the cells contract, they flex tiny piezoelectric generators spread across the mesh, and that produces electricity. The researchers say the device reached more than 10 times the effective energy density of conventional energy-harvesting approaches in lab tests. It's published in Science Advances.

00:06:12.013 --> 00:06:34.877
<v Casey>Ben Hardill wanted to read a pair of IKEA ALPSTUGA CO2 sensors from Node-RED. IKEA's second-generation devices use Thread instead of Zigbee, so his old hub was no use. He bought a Smlight SLZB-06MU border router, added Bluetooth commissioning to a Matter controller node, and opened a pull request. The code is on GitHub, which hosts this show's code too. He has both sensors logging to InfluxDB now.

00:06:36.466 --> 00:06:55.226
<v Riley>Gar's Tips &amp; Tools, issue 216, recommends a three-pack of cheap plastic hex-key T-handles, under $2 each, that grip well for people with arthritic hands. The same issue has a Laura Kampf video about a pocket rolling measuring disk, prototyped on a forthcoming desktop CNC, the Infimaker 5-axis.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:15.548
<v Casey>Someone rooted a 2018 Echo Dot to cut it loose from Amazon's cloud, then turned it into a Home Assistant voice satellite. The project it runs is written with AI coding help, and the writer says it's unaudited and works only on certain Echo devices. On a Dot, you first have to solder a USB connector onto test pads on the board.

00:07:17.005 --> 00:07:37.509
<v Riley>A maker reused the motherboard of an eight-year-old Xiaomi Mi 8, bought used for $23 and running LineageOS. They used its LTE connection as a long-range video and control link for an RC plane. The phone's back glass is cracked, and a workaround in the device tree stops the boot from timing out when the display is missing.

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:53.070
<v Casey>NINASENSE nRF is an open-source board with Bluetooth Low Energy and LoRa radios, designed to run for months or years on two AAA batteries. Its companion software lets you program it without writing code. It measures 57 by 28 millimetres.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.912
<v Riley>Two AAA batteries for years is the number a remote sensor lives or dies on.

00:07:59.697 --> 00:08:15.945
<v Riley>Jan de Jager has published e486-web, an Intel 80486DX2-66 PC emulator written in JavaScript. It runs in a browser with no dependencies and no build step. It boots real firmware, then FreeDOS 1.3, and runs DOS games including DOOM.

00:08:17.531 --> 00:08:31.379
<v Casey>Many ESP32 boards carry PSRAM that beginner tutorials rarely mention. It helps with camera frame buffers and audio decoding, but a simple temperature-and-humidity sensor doesn't need it, and only certain boards, such as the ESP32-S3, have it.

00:08:32.893 --> 00:08:57.957
<v Riley>Ken Shirriff opened up the 1980 Intel 8087 math coprocessor and photographed the die under a microscope. He walks through how it does floating-point addition and subtraction, using a microcode ROM with 1,648 micro-instructions and a 69-bit integer adder. The chip sped up floating point in the IBM PC roughly 100 times over emulation on the 8086 for addition.

00:08:58.407 --> 00:09:02.007
<v Riley>The Duo screens are a parts question, and we'll get to that one shortly.

00:09:03.107 --> 00:09:21.299
<v Casey>Community Futures Cariboo Chilcotin is a nonprofit with a volunteer board that supports rural entrepreneurs and community economic development. If you run a small shop, a repair business, or you're thinking about starting one in the Cariboo, it's a good place to start a conversation. You can find them at cfdccariboo.com.

00:09:21.823 --> 00:09:27.495
<v Riley>A repair business needs someone to talk to before it needs a loan. Good to know who's at that table.

00:09:27.595 --> 00:09:28.931
<v Riley>Now to the parts question.

00:09:30.031 --> 00:09:57.055
<v Riley>Start with the rule that aims straight at repair. The EU's rules for phones and tablets have applied since June 20th, 2025. Manufacturers have to supply key spare parts within five to ten working days, and keep them available for at least seven years after a model leaves the EU market. Every model gets a repairability score from A to E, and the maker has to publish free repair instructions and spare-parts prices.

00:09:57.639 --> 00:10:11.543
<v Casey>A year in, TechRadar spoke to three experts. They see real improvement, and they still question how hard and how expensive repairs are. Those two things decide whether someone in Horsefly ever gets a fix. Better on paper doesn't put a part in your hand.

00:10:12.202 --> 00:10:25.618
<v Riley>Paper is where every rule starts. The question underneath all of this: when a government reshapes what gear people can buy, does a remote owner end up with parts and repairs within reach, or with a different set of gatekeepers?

00:10:25.913 --> 00:10:31.489
<v Casey>That's the one. Let's test it on the repair rule first, because it's the only one that's about repair.

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:57.698
<v Riley>On October 7th we argued about repairability scores that miss what owners live with. This time the gap sits behind the score. A one-year audit by Right to Repair Europe, republished by iFixit on September 10th, checked 2,334 smartphone records in the EU's energy label registry. Only about 18 percent listed a web address where you could readily find parts prices or repair instructions.

00:10:57.991 --> 00:11:00.122
<v Casey>So four out of five gave you nothing to click.

00:11:00.122 --> 00:11:13.794
<v Riley>Roughly. About half had blank web fields. Around 19 percent pointed to pages with neither prices nor instructions. Close to 5 percent pointed at Temu or AliExpress, and some non-compliant models still self-declared an A.

00:11:14.129 --> 00:11:18.025
<v Casey>A link to a Temu listing. Does that count as repair information at all?

00:11:18.334 --> 00:11:30.918
<v Riley>A blank field is only visible once a field is mandatory. Before June 2025 nobody was counting the blanks, so the audit turns a hunch into a number. Eighteen percent is partly a measure of how early it is.

00:11:31.594 --> 00:11:38.522
<v Casey>Counting blanks doesn't fix them. The audit also found member states don't have the staff to police this. Consumer groups are doing the checking.

00:11:39.106 --> 00:11:47.042
<v Riley>And the consumer groups found it. Mandatory disclosure gives the checkers something to check. That's the mechanism working, slowly.

00:11:47.664 --> 00:11:55.536
<v Casey>An A score next to a dead link is a label misleading the buyer, which is the one thing a label is for. Without enforcement it's a paper right.

00:11:55.861 --> 00:11:58.373
<v Riley>Enforce the label, don't scrap the score.

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:08.162
<v Casey>The EU also kept parts prices off the score itself. France puts them on it, and campaigners asked for the same here. A separate web page is one more click for the buyer.

00:12:08.784 --> 00:12:18.016
<v Riley>I prefer France's version too. France also has a repair bonus, which changes what a customer pays at the counter. The EU version is weaker, not a failure.

00:12:18.116 --> 00:12:27.084
<v Riley>The Apple and Samsung pages are compliant, by the audit's reading. They just take several clicks to reach. So the rule is being met on a technicality.

00:12:27.653 --> 00:12:34.637
<v Casey>Several clicks on a phone with a cracked screen. Anyone who's tried to read a manual on a broken display knows how that goes.

00:12:34.737 --> 00:12:37.537
<v Casey>Does a buyer compare battery prices before picking a phone?

00:12:37.793 --> 00:12:44.809
<v Riley>Not at the till. The repair shop does, two years later, when the battery is dead. That's who the seven-year window is for.

00:12:45.422 --> 00:12:54.662
<v Casey>Two years later, in a town where the shop is a long drive, and the part has to be ordered in. The window is seven years. The drive is the same length it was on day one.

00:12:55.269 --> 00:13:00.645
<v Riley>Then the five-to-ten working day clock matters most for that buyer. A regulator can check it.

00:13:01.171 --> 00:13:08.539
<v Casey>A clock starts when the manufacturer decides it does. I'd want to see a part delivered to a rural address before I'd call it a clock.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:16.292
<v Riley>Nobody has published delivery times to rural addresses, as far as the sources go. That's a gap, and an easy one to measure.

00:13:16.812 --> 00:13:29.652
<v Casey>Now the other side of the Atlantic, where the rules aren't about repair at all. The FCC put foreign-produced drones and consumer routers on its Covered List, and has since extended that to foreign-made robots, including robot vacuums.

00:13:30.183 --> 00:13:42.079
<v Riley>The starting point was network security, and that worry is real. The Verge reports the first move was a December 2025 ban on all future foreign drones unless a company obtains a waiver. Routers followed.

00:13:42.629 --> 00:13:51.577
<v Casey>Look at rip and replace. Telecoms were told to swap out Huawei and ZTE gear, and many still haven't. Swapping hardware on a government timetable fails in practice.

00:13:51.577 --> 00:14:03.041
<v Riley>These bans cover future devices. Whatever a household already owns stays in the field. Mashable reports banned drones and routers can still get critical updates until 2029.

00:14:03.615 --> 00:14:12.639
<v Casey>That's press coverage, not the order. I wouldn't tell a household its router is safe until I've read the text. And nothing in what we have says a word about parts.

00:14:13.285 --> 00:14:23.725
<v Riley>Take the robot vacuum. Consumer Reports and Mashable note that Roborock, Ecovacs and Dreame are Chinese brands. iRobot is American, and it manufactures largely in China and Vietnam.

00:14:24.282 --> 00:14:32.866
<v Casey>So "American" on the box doesn't tell you where the parts come from. A brand name is a marketing decision, and a bill of materials is a different document.

00:14:33.469 --> 00:14:57.333
<v Riley>Which is where Anduril and Qualcomm come in. They announced BlueChip, a laptop built on Snapdragon X Series chips, with the first model planned for 2027. The Verge says the prototype is about 32 percent U.S.-origin by component procurement cost. Anduril now says it will reach at least 65 percent U.S.-made components by launch, which is the Buy American threshold.

00:14:57.864 --> 00:15:11.768
<v Casey>32 percent is what's on the bench. 65 is a promise with a date on it. And the baselines differ: procurement cost, component count and "allied" sourcing aren't the same measure. TechRadar also warns of a roughly 20 percent price premium.

00:15:12.318 --> 00:15:26.334
<v Riley>One model is how a supply chain starts. TechCrunch reports the CPU is built at TSMC's Arizona facility. A domestic chip with auditable parts is a real foundation, and a maker building at home could design for local service.

00:15:26.896 --> 00:15:39.408
<v Casey>It's aimed at government and military buyers, and it's rugged. Rugged usually means sealed. None of the reporting says BlueChip will be repairable, and a 2027 launch gives a buyer in the Cariboo nothing to fix today.

00:15:39.732 --> 00:15:43.108
<v Riley>Sealed and rugged are design choices. Those can change.

00:15:43.783 --> 00:15:58.263
<v Casey>Can change, and no one has said they will. On August 12th we argued that predictive maintenance is useless if you're barred from actually fixing what fails. Same logic applies to a robot vacuum. A promise of parts doesn't help if the owner can't open the thing.

00:15:58.763 --> 00:16:03.731
<v Riley>Locked service keys and manuals are the same barrier. Repair law has to touch both.

00:16:04.412 --> 00:16:09.180
<v Casey>Both, and in the US actions there's no repair law in view. That's what I keep returning to.

00:16:09.797 --> 00:16:22.765
<v Riley>So the question sharpens. The EU effort is about repair difficulty and cost. The US actions are about import origin and a future domestic laptop. Neither piece joins them, and a remote owner benefits only where they meet.

00:16:23.325 --> 00:16:31.981
<v Casey>If security rules decide what gets sold and nobody attaches a repair requirement, remote owners could end up with fewer options and no more fixability.

00:16:32.548 --> 00:16:44.548
<v Riley>The EU shows repair rules can stand on their own and still change what's on shelves. Joining them isn't a precondition. The EU's parts window already reshapes what a manufacturer ships into Europe.

00:16:45.208 --> 00:16:52.784
<v Casey>The EU's rule is a repair rule. The US actions are an import rule. Different instrument, different result, and a different person paying.

00:16:53.381 --> 00:17:05.853
<v Riley>Take a household in Quesnel, or out past Horsefly, with a robot vacuum, a router and a phone that's three years old. Slow arrival is still arrival. A market shaped by big rules widens the options for small towns too.

00:17:06.488 --> 00:17:16.808
<v Casey>Slow arrival is also the drive to the shop, the wait for the part, and a counter that may not stock it. A household that waits for a rule to filter north pays for that trip in the meantime.

00:17:17.343 --> 00:17:22.775
<v Riley>Nobody's arguing the trip is free. The argument is whether the trip gets cheaper over time.

00:17:23.402 --> 00:17:29.506
<v Casey>It only gets cheaper when someone is responsible for it getting cheaper. Right now, in these US actions, nobody is.

00:17:30.095 --> 00:17:37.871
<v Riley>The EU's parts window covers products sold in the EU. As far as the sources go, nothing requires the same window for Cariboo buyers.

00:17:38.534 --> 00:17:47.270
<v Casey>Field gear sharpens it. That Czech solar light from the roundup is what gear looks like when nobody is checking. Off-grid kit lives on the edges of the rules.

00:17:47.801 --> 00:17:59.169
<v Riley>The Muzi Works Base Uno from earlier is the opposite case. A pre-flashed board with a solar input could be serviced by someone with a basic kit. Whether it gets serviced is a separate matter.

00:17:59.726 --> 00:18:07.174
<v Casey>A node in the hills with a bad battery and no one nearby who knows how to open it. That's the same problem with a different antenna.

00:18:07.274 --> 00:18:21.730
<v Casey>So the landing. Should a rule that reshapes what can be sold also carry a repair requirement for parts and service access? Or should repair live in separate law, with the risk that security rules leave owners with fewer options and no more ability to fix them?

00:18:22.026 --> 00:18:26.682
<v Riley>Build it in. The repair requirement belongs on the rule that decides what gets sold.

00:18:27.325 --> 00:18:34.861
<v Casey>Then who checks it? The EU's gaps were found by consumer groups, not by the regulator. I'm not convinced until a part shows up in Quesnel.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:51.096
<v Riley>Then check your own phone. Look up the repair page for your model and count the clicks. Anyone can run that test before the next purchase. And if you have a correction, a story tip, or want to get involved, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We read all of it.

00:18:51.671 --> 00:18:58.535
<v Casey>And if your page sends you to a listing on Temu, you have your answer. Watch that drizzle this morning, it's sitting right around freezing.

00:18:58.715 --> 00:18:59.595
<v Riley>Have a good Wednesday.

00:19:00.195 --> 00:19:19.267
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.